        "title": "Lemonade went from 30% to 98% AI-assisted PRs ≡ƒñ» Few weeks ago, I went to their \"Building AI-Native Systems\" meetup to see how. Erez Dickman and Dor Tzur opened with Lemonade's agentic coding journey. The MCP and skills/commands ecosystem is what made the difference. The more tools you give your agent, the more thorough the result. This is something we see more and more in the last couple of months, and that's why there's a lot of effort from all providers into improving the models' tools utilization. Some examples of the MCPs Lemonade uses: ΓÇó ≡¥ùª≡¥ù╗≡¥ù╝≡¥ÿä≡¥ù│≡¥ù╣≡¥ù«≡¥ù╕≡¥ù▓ ≡¥ùá≡¥ùû≡¥ùú - for fetching prod data. Any data warehouse MCP would work here. ΓÇó ≡¥ùù≡¥ù«≡¥ÿü≡¥ù«≡¥ù▒≡¥ù╝≡¥ù┤ ≡¥ùá≡¥ùû≡¥ùú - for production investigations. Could also be Sentry, Logz.io , etc. ΓÇó ≡¥ùÖ≡¥ù╢≡¥ù»≡¥ù▓≡¥ù┐≡¥ÿå ≡¥ùá≡¥ùû≡¥ùú - for giving the agent task context. Same idea as connecting Linear or Jira. ΓÇó ≡¥ùû≡¥ù£/≡¥ùû≡¥ùù ≡¥ùá≡¥ùû≡¥ùú - for investigating pipeline failures. ΓÇó ≡¥ùƒ≡¥ùá≡¥ùû≡¥ùú - their own internal MCP for company-specific operations (dev env setup, feature flags, deployment flows). Dor Tzur only sent 10 messages in the entire session. That's how investigation time went from a day to an hour, which is mind blowing honestly. Lemonade, like many companies, wants to know whether AI truly boosts their productivity. They created and open sourced a complexity-analyzer (link in comments) that scores PRs on a 1-10 scale, so you can filter out the noise and focus on the PRs that actually matter when measuring AI's impact, which I think is really awesome. Shay Davidson presented their architecture for handling human and AI collaboration on their claim handling solution at scale which was really awesome to see. The way I understood it was that they have same task structure whether a human or an AI agent executes it (with git like phases), you can require human sign-off, gate it behind a feature flag, or let it auto-promote once precision is high enough. They reached 95% precision on new task automations in under 10 days ≡ƒñ» The talk that stuck with me most was by Ofir Ventura and Daniel Korn about AskQL, an internal data chatbot that allows different departments among Lemonade to query data using a conversational flow. The agents runs on a ReAct loop (Reasoning + Acting) using LlamaIndex . They also built different modes for different users. Data Analysts get an open ended \"Explore\" mode across raw Snowflake data. Ops Managers get a structured flow with pre defined metrics. To handle trust, they built a golden dataset (expected questions, expected SQL, expected answers) and collect user feedback on every response. Which MCPs/Skills do you use daily? Let me know in the comments. Looking forward to the next meetups, hoping things will be calmer here soon. #AI #MCP #Skills #Meetups #AIEngineering #DeveloperExperience",
